; CollectionTrail.inix -- how this database opens, and how each table is heard.
;
; ONE DATABASE FOR WHATEVER YOU COLLECT. Stamps, coins, records, tools, anything
; else -- they all answer the same questions, so they share one table, items, and
; the collection field says which collection a thing belongs to. Keep them all in
; one file and sort by collection, or make a copy for each; the shape is the same
; either way.
;
; The fields are the ones a collector of anything needs: what it is, who made it,
; when and where it is from, an identifier the hobby uses (a catalogue number, a
; mint mark, a pressing), condition, how many, when and where you got it, what
; you paid, what it is worth, where it is now, and whether it is stored, on show,
; lent out, sold, or still wanted.
;
; F4 in collection, condition, source or status picks from the values in use, so
; a collection is chosen rather than typed again. Add your own to the lookups
; table.
;
; WHEN A COLLECTION NEEDS MORE, make it its own database. CellarTrail is the
; worked example: wine wants a vintage, a drinking window and a bottle count that
; falls as you drink, which no general shape gives you.

[Database]

InitialTable = items

[Table:items]

; The collection first, so one press of its letter reaches it, then the thing
; itself and who made it.
SelectFields = collection, item, maker, year
OrderFields = collection, item
WhereFilter =

[Table:lookups]

SelectFields = fld, val
OrderFields = fld, ordinal
WhereFilter =

[Table:maps]

SelectFields = kind, prime1, prime2
OrderFields = kind, prime1
WhereFilter =
